<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">British Togoland</h1><p class="st-meta">1916 CE – 1956 CE · capital Ho</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The western part of German Togoland, ruled by Britain from the Gold Coast, whose 1956 plebiscite made it the first trust territory to join an independent state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> divided <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-togoland">German Togoland</a> in 1916, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> taking a strip along the western border with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony</a>. In 1922 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> confirmed it as a British mandate, and after 1946 it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trust territory. It included part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewe-people">Ewe</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> and northern areas linked to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dagbon">Dagbon</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mamprugu">Mamprugu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> administered the territory as part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast</a>, the north with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-territories-of-the-gold-coast">Northern Territories of the Gold Coast</a> and the south from the colony, with Ho as the main southern centre. Cocoa farming spread in the south. The division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewe-people">Ewe</a> between British <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-togoland">Togoland</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-togoland">French Togoland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> fed an Ewe unification movement that petitioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In a UN plebiscite on 9 May 1956 a majority voted for union with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast</a>, though most southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewe-people">Ewe</a> districts voted against. The territory became part of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> in 1957; its southern part formed the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta</a> Region, while the north joined the Northern Region.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-togoland.jpg" alt="British Togoland" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Original: Acts of Union 1800 Vector: Yaddah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(3-5).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1916 CE – 1956 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ho</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ewe, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Togoland under British Mandate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=british-togoland" aria-label="British Togoland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4693 1930 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where British Togoland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5013 2186L5034 2204M5013 2186L4997 651M5013 2186L4995 2226"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5034 2204h0M4997 651h0M4995 2226h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5013 2186h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5013 2186h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ho. <a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=british-togoland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
